WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.132 --> 00:00:05.251
[MUSIC]

00:00:05.251 --> 00:00:08.448
I'm in the locker room with my
Washington University and St.

00:00:08.448 --> 00:00:10.640
Louis basketball teammates.

00:00:10.640 --> 00:00:14.746
Blasting the only
acceptable pump up song for

00:00:14.746 --> 00:00:18.643
2011, Jason Derulo's Riding Solo.

00:00:18.643 --> 00:00:22.024
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I'm a freshman point guard trying to

00:00:22.024 --> 00:00:24.605
take it all in and
doing my best not to mess up.

00:00:24.605 --> 00:00:30.313
The game that we're getting ready for
is the one we've been waiting for

00:00:30.313 --> 00:00:34.416
our entire lives,
a national championship game.

00:00:34.416 --> 00:00:38.085
As we ran out of the tunnel onto
the court, I anticipated the usual

00:00:38.085 --> 00:00:41.640
adrenaline rush that accompanies
the sound of a rising crowd.

00:00:43.350 --> 00:00:48.427
But the mild applause we
received was a total buzz kill.

00:00:48.427 --> 00:00:55.178
Besides our families and a few loyal
super fans, the stands were mostly empty.

00:00:55.178 --> 00:00:57.617
Our National Championship game,

00:00:57.617 --> 00:01:02.249
turned into something like
a practice scrimmage with referees.

00:01:02.249 --> 00:01:06.379
I don't like showing pictures from
that game because the empty stands in

00:01:06.379 --> 00:01:09.100
the background make me
feel self-conscious.

00:01:09.100 --> 00:01:12.450
We lost that game, but
I walked away with a deeper sadness.

00:01:14.450 --> 00:01:18.190
This is the reality for
many women athletes today.

00:01:18.190 --> 00:01:22.640
They spend years and dedication to their
craft, but their amazing abilities and

00:01:22.640 --> 00:01:25.050
accomplishments go under appreciated.

00:01:26.450 --> 00:01:29.419
What a serious missed opportunity.

00:01:29.419 --> 00:01:34.357
Every person, sports fan or not,
can appreciate the beauty and

00:01:34.357 --> 00:01:36.651
promise of women's sports.

00:01:36.651 --> 00:01:38.790
You should watch women's sports.

00:01:38.790 --> 00:01:39.780
You won't be disappointed.

00:01:41.460 --> 00:01:44.140
Let's take a step back and
think about how we got here.

00:01:45.160 --> 00:01:48.190
Three damaging myths
surrounding women's sports

00:01:48.190 --> 00:01:49.970
deserve to be redressed and debunked.

00:01:51.010 --> 00:01:55.940
Women athletes aren't as talented as men,
women's sports are boring, and

00:01:55.940 --> 00:01:57.320
women's sports don't matter.

00:01:58.680 --> 00:02:00.120
Let's start with the first one.

00:02:00.120 --> 00:02:02.200
Women athletes aren't as talented as men.

00:02:03.450 --> 00:02:05.060
Under this rationale,

00:02:05.060 --> 00:02:09.580
if Elena Delle Donne can't dunk like
LeBron James, it's not worth watching.

00:02:10.900 --> 00:02:16.160
If Allyson Felix can't beat Justin Gatlin
in a head to head 100m dash,

00:02:16.160 --> 00:02:17.190
it's not worth watching.

00:02:18.690 --> 00:02:23.480
While it is true that men have larger
muscle mass that makes them stronger and

00:02:23.480 --> 00:02:26.710
longer arms and
legs that make them faster,

00:02:26.710 --> 00:02:29.250
it's also true that there
are other markers of athleticism.

00:02:30.490 --> 00:02:36.880
Being a good athlete is about skill,
finesse, IQ, determination,

00:02:36.880 --> 00:02:41.500
and sportsmanship all of which women
hold in just as large quantity as men.

00:02:43.090 --> 00:02:47.010
Let's take a look at some of the most
talented women athletes I know.

00:02:47.010 --> 00:02:52.364
You've probably heard of Serena Williams,
Simone Biles, and Megan Rapinoe.

00:02:52.364 --> 00:02:55.168
But I'd like to introduce you to
three you might not have heard of.

00:02:55.168 --> 00:03:02.016
First, Gwen Jorgensen,she's the 2016
Olympic gold medalist in triathlon.

00:03:02.016 --> 00:03:03.857
She was an accountant at Ernst and
Young and

00:03:03.857 --> 00:03:07.400
left her jobs become a full
time professional triathlete.

00:03:07.400 --> 00:03:11.785
She's now looking to become the first
American woman to win gold in the marathon

00:03:11.785 --> 00:03:12.771
since 1984.

00:03:14.481 --> 00:03:20.019
Claressa Shields, two-time Olympic
gold medalist in women's boxing.

00:03:20.019 --> 00:03:23.074
Claressa has a really inspiring story,
she grew up in Flint,

00:03:23.074 --> 00:03:27.390
Michigan in a childhood that was marked by
poverty, abuse, and a speech impediment.

00:03:30.080 --> 00:03:33.990
And finally, Kate Courtney,
top American mountain biker.

00:03:33.990 --> 00:03:35.200
She went here, to Stanford.

00:03:36.860 --> 00:03:39.990
Kate loves sharks and Shark Week.

00:03:39.990 --> 00:03:44.370
In fact, on her bike, where it says
her name, she's changed the a in Kate

00:03:44.370 --> 00:03:48.960
to resemble a shark fin because she says
that's what her riding style is like.

00:03:48.960 --> 00:03:52.730
She lurks beneath the surface and then
she pounces right when the time is right.

00:03:55.580 --> 00:03:59.246
Stories like these,
of talented women with fun and

00:03:59.246 --> 00:04:05.313
inspiring backgrounds abound in women's
sports, we just don't hear about them.

00:04:05.313 --> 00:04:09.390
Only 4% of sports media coverage
goes to women's sports.

00:04:11.160 --> 00:04:14.740
So it's not that women
athletes aren't talented.

00:04:14.740 --> 00:04:18.310
It's that we don't have enough exposure
to be able to assess talent accurately.

00:04:20.120 --> 00:04:24.206
Second, women's sports are boring.

00:04:24.206 --> 00:04:29.311
I'll turn that on its head and
ask, what even is boredom?

00:04:29.311 --> 00:04:32.277
Because if you're bored
watching some of these events,

00:04:32.277 --> 00:04:34.324
it's not a problem with the athletes.

00:04:34.324 --> 00:04:40.480
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I want to

00:04:40.480 --> 00:04:44.783
talk about one of my favorite women's
sporting events from recent history,

00:04:44.783 --> 00:04:48.840
the 2018 Olympic final
in cross-country skiing.

00:04:48.840 --> 00:04:49.680
The American skier,

00:04:49.680 --> 00:04:53.200
Jessie Diggins, rounded the curve
to come down the home stretch.

00:04:53.200 --> 00:04:55.120
She was a few lengths behind.

00:04:55.120 --> 00:04:59.862
And she put on one of the most powerful
performances I've ever seen to cross

00:04:59.862 --> 00:05:04.092
the finish line and win Olympic
gold in this photographic finish.

00:05:04.092 --> 00:05:09.205
Or how about Nneka Ogwumike, Stanford
grad, who hit this game winning shot as

00:05:09.205 --> 00:05:14.177
she felt to her back to win the WNBA
championship for the Los Angeles Sparks.

00:05:16.468 --> 00:05:21.823
So you're going to tell me that
watching NASCAR cars- &gt; [LAUGH]

00:05:21.823 --> 00:05:26.685
&gt;&gt; Go around in circles 200 times in a row

00:05:26.685 --> 00:05:30.120
is more exciting than this?

00:05:30.120 --> 00:05:33.836
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Or that ESPN should

00:05:33.836 --> 00:05:36.557
put professional corn hole on-
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:05:36.557 --> 00:05:38.144
&gt;&gt; Instead of professional women's soccer.

00:05:40.640 --> 00:05:44.377
The excitement around
women's sports is there.

00:05:44.377 --> 00:05:46.222
It's up to us to pay attention.

00:05:48.133 --> 00:05:51.776
Women's Sports, don't matter.

00:05:51.776 --> 00:05:58.250
The World Series and Super Bowl captivate
men's sports fans for weeks at a time.

00:05:58.250 --> 00:06:02.230
Because women's sports doesn't have
the infrastructure or the media coverage,

00:06:02.230 --> 00:06:05.420
it can be difficult to know when the big
games and competitions are happening.

00:06:06.470 --> 00:06:08.390
But that doesn't mean that
women's sports don't matter.

00:06:09.960 --> 00:06:14.130
Girls and women who play sports have
higher levels of self confidence,

00:06:14.130 --> 00:06:17.280
higher motivation to achieve
long term goals, and

00:06:17.280 --> 00:06:20.900
lower levels of obesity,
diabetes, and certain cancers.

00:06:25.820 --> 00:06:29.100
Playing sports can translate
into off field success as well.

00:06:29.100 --> 00:06:29.980
Research from Ernst and

00:06:29.980 --> 00:06:34.865
Young and ESPN shows that of senior
level business women in America,

00:06:34.865 --> 00:06:40.610
94% have a background in sport, and 52%
of them competed at the collegiate level.

00:06:41.880 --> 00:06:44.350
So if you care about
the future of our country and

00:06:44.350 --> 00:06:48.630
want to see a more equitable world,
you'll know women's sports matter.

00:06:50.210 --> 00:06:54.340
Professional women athletes are also some
of the most amazing advocates out there.

00:06:54.340 --> 00:06:58.020
You might have heard of the US
soccer teams fight for equal pay.

00:06:58.020 --> 00:07:03.115
But did you know that the US hockey team
went through a similar battle in 2017?

00:07:03.115 --> 00:07:07.666
When Colin Kaepernick knelt to protest
systemic racism in our country,

00:07:07.666 --> 00:07:11.855
US soccer star Megan Rapinoe was
the first to kneel in solidarity.

00:07:11.855 --> 00:07:17.141
And arguably the best basketball player
in the world, Maya Moore, has taken

00:07:17.141 --> 00:07:22.427
two years away from the game to advocate
for prison reform, specifically for

00:07:22.427 --> 00:07:27.493
a man she believes is wrongly convicted
and sits on death row in Missouri.

00:07:27.493 --> 00:07:31.950
Once you know these women and
their stories, it's hard not to care.

00:07:33.410 --> 00:07:37.300
The same way that we draft and
follow our fantasy football leagues, or

00:07:37.300 --> 00:07:42.210
latch onto contestants in the Bachelor,
that's what I want for women's sports.

00:07:43.680 --> 00:07:47.648
Which brings me to what you
can do to better support.

00:07:47.648 --> 00:07:51.190
At Stanford, it could mean watching one or
two events live.

00:07:51.190 --> 00:07:56.290
We have some of the most amazing athletes
in the world right here on our campus.

00:07:56.290 --> 00:08:01.580
In the last year, our women's soccer,
volleyball, swimming and diving,

00:08:01.580 --> 00:08:06.400
rowing, water polo, and tennis teams
have all won national championships.

00:08:06.400 --> 00:08:08.282
That's six in the last year.

00:08:08.282 --> 00:08:15.731
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]
&gt;&gt; When you're in a bar or

00:08:15.731 --> 00:08:21.563
restaurant, ask the server to turn one
of the TVs to a women's sporting event.

00:08:21.563 --> 00:08:25.548
Even if you're only casually watching
the game, other patrons will stroll in and

00:08:25.548 --> 00:08:28.680
see that it's on TV and
it will start to become more normalized.

00:08:30.928 --> 00:08:36.007
I can't understate the importance
of stories in women's sports.

00:08:36.007 --> 00:08:40.040
Invest in learning about a player or
a team and follow them for a season.

00:08:40.040 --> 00:08:42.390
Maybe it's someone who's from
your hometown or home state or

00:08:42.390 --> 00:08:44.440
someone who speaks out about
a cause you care about.

00:08:45.520 --> 00:08:47.873
Once you're invested in their success,

00:08:47.873 --> 00:08:50.908
I think you'll find it's
hard not to pay attention.

00:08:50.908 --> 00:08:54.830
Another thing you can do is consider being
a coach for a local youth girls team.

00:08:56.090 --> 00:08:58.810
Being a constant presence in a young
girl's life can make a world

00:08:58.810 --> 00:08:59.310
of difference.

00:09:01.350 --> 00:09:05.239
And finally, as business school students,
I would remiss

00:09:05.239 --> 00:09:10.161
not to plant a seed that maybe one day
you should buy a women's sports team.

00:09:10.161 --> 00:09:16.493
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
[APPLAUSE]

00:09:16.493 --> 00:09:18.539
&gt;&gt; And grow it into the most successful

00:09:18.539 --> 00:09:21.206
sports franchise in
the history of the world.

00:09:21.206 --> 00:09:26.155
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I still have a recurring dream.

00:09:26.155 --> 00:09:29.189
It happens about once a month or so.

00:09:29.189 --> 00:09:31.521
I'm back in college,
this time as a senior.

00:09:31.521 --> 00:09:36.234
I'm in that locker room getting ready for
another national championship game.

00:09:36.234 --> 00:09:39.436
But this time,
when I run out onto the court,

00:09:39.436 --> 00:09:43.833
I'm met with a wall of applause
that sends me back on my heels.

00:09:43.833 --> 00:09:46.254
I want this to be more than a dream.

00:09:46.254 --> 00:09:49.726
I want it to be the reality for
the next generation of female athletes.

00:09:49.726 --> 00:09:50.388
Thank you.

00:09:50.388 --> 00:09:52.325
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:09:52.325 --> 00:09:57.286
[MUSIC]

